>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Ms. Davis: One thing I find interesting about these true crime novels is that the antagonists are more relatable than I expected. These serial killers are not simply black and white, good or evil, guilty or innocent. The reason this book is so uncomfortable is that we get the whole story about the murderer. He has a difficult upbringing and serious mental health issues. There are times when he is humanized, and it feels really uncomfortable. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (JL: How does point of view in these books impact us as readers?)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Ms. Davis: Another thing I think is very interesting about good literature is that the author can manipulate you. The book is written from many perspectives. You move from the crime scene, disgusted by the brutality, to the mom who lost her son, feeling angry and sad. You are drawn to wherever the author wants to take you. The POV of each book or documentary depends on the author or director, and how the story is presented really depends on what they want you to know.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
